00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:39.530
We need to recombine
them into a 28

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:42.415
by 28 image, and the
standard way of--

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:48.490
when you do a standard
reshape, it actually

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:52.490
ends up with something that is
transposed and isn't a number.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:55.240
So that's supposed to be a
k, just as FYI, in case it

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:55.810
wasn't clear.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:57.460
Not pi, k.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:00.040
And it's because
they're transposed,

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.410
so you want it to
look like that.

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:05.320
And so this is a good
opportunity for us

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:07.540
to illustrate a little
bit of preprocessing.

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:12.130
I'm just basically pulling
out the data, reshaping it,

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:16.650
and then calling numpy.transpose
and flipping the two axes--

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.230
the zeroth and first axes,
the first and zeroth.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.160
And that gives us
our flipped result.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:26.832
So we'll have do that every
time we want to show the image.

00:18:26.832 --> 00:18:28.790
So I made a little function
to help us do that.

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.780
So now you don't have
to write that yourself.

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:34.510
The other piece of it
is understanding, like,

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.810
what we said earlier, that
the class number or the class

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:40.930
label, class 10, is
actually a capital A.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:44.830
So when I'm naming these you
