00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:12.730
Read in the file.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:16.650
And then here I'm just
setting some values

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:18.480
in terms of the
dimension of the image.

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:23.190
I could just skip that and
just use hardcoded values.

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.680
And then we have our transpose.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.440
Here the transpose is a little
more involved, because when

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:31.530
you actually grab the full
dataset and not just one image,

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:39.770
it's 112,000 rows
by 768 or 769--

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:41.850
one extra in the front.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:45.720
So we have to just
flip the middle two,

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.890
because the first one
is the batch size.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.650
So that's 112,800.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:51.570
And the last one is
the dimensionality,

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:52.950
in this case one.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:54.040
And so we switch them.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:59.670
So instead of being 0,
1, 2, 3 it's 0, 2, 1, 3.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:06.270
And then the final thing is
just kind of a bookkeeping step

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:11.620
to switch our labels to one
hot encoded just for the Keras

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.590
model to be able to
read that in properly.

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:17.830
So that changes it from being
1, 2, 3, 4 all the way to 46,

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:22.740
or zero through 46, to being
an array of 46 values, one

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:25.620
of which is a one and the others
are zeroes, the index of which

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:30.330
corresponds to the value.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.760
Oh yes, and because these are
images, the number of times

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:37.590
I have forgotten to divide
by 255 for grayscale images,

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:40.980
