bunch of arguments--

00:25:09.234 --> 00:25:11.150
I'm going to make this
smaller by a little bit

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:12.983
just so we can see the
arguments more easily

00:25:12.983 --> 00:25:16.550
and it doesn't new
line itself to death.

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:19.580
The standard one I'm just
saying, just train it,

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:23.480
give a 20%, 80% validation
split of the training

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:28.490
data, whereas with this one you
can have it alter the image,

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:32.210
because they are images
and because we reshaped it

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:32.750
at the top.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:36.140
Rather than passing them
in as arrays of 768,

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:39.560
we are actually passing
in 28 by 28 images.

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:43.340
So we can pass in all this
stuff around shifting the image

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:46.640
around, as well as
rotation, zoom, and shearing

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:48.590
of the image, the tweak.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:52.880
And so that sort of stuff can
help introduce more variety

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:55.550
into your dataset if your
dataset isn't super large.

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:58.790
In this case, I have
112,000 examples.

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:01.220
I figured that's
probably OK for now.

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:04.540
And so I opted to just use
the normal data generator,

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:09.390
but thought this might be
handy, so I left it in here.

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:11.450
And so we pull out
our two generators.

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:14.120
So we call the generator.flow
and get our training

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:17.450
and validation generators,
and pass that in to do

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:20.150
model.fit_generator.

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:23.270
And for a while I
was doing 10 epochs.

00:26:23.270 --> 00:26:25.600
10 epochs does work pretty
well here in terms of,

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:27.020
it gets much further along.

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:30.240
The sixth through
10th epoch do matter.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:32.840
I just made it five for a
while, and probably forgot

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:35.384
to change it back because
it just runs faster, right?

00:26:35.384 --> 00:26:36.800
And so when I was
testing I didn't

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.450
want to have it run forever.

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:42.320
So with five epochs
we got to 71%.

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.790
With five more, we're
closing in on 80.

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:46.790
And for this being
kind of a back

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:50.420
of the envelope, random
network, it's not the worst.

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:53.360
It's of course, as you
mentioned, certainly not

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:56.030
the best network in the world.

00:26:56.030 --> 00:27:01.390
And it's certainly not trained
to any crazy degree either.

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:04.640
So at this point we have
a trained model, right?

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.610
And then we can call evaluate
and pass in our test data

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:10.400
generator that we made here.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:15.560
The valuation, I'm always
impressed at how concise

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:16.250
that can be.

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:19.066
It's very similar to the
training code in that we

00:27:19.066 --> 00:27:21.440
load in the data the same way,
just passing the test data

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:23.075
path instead of the
train data path,

00:27:23.075 --> 00:27:26.422
and then the data generator is
very straightforward to call

00:27:26.422 --> 00:27:28.880
and there's not much in the
way of arguments since when you

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:30.650
do evaluation you
only need to pass

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:33.740
through the dataset one time
instead of looping it over

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:35.067
and over and over.

00:27:35.067 --> 00:27:36.650
The numbers that are
coming out here--

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:39.110
the first number is the
loss, and the second number

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:40.380
is the accuracy.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:43.400
So on our test dataset,
we're getting about 77%,

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:47.249
which is kind of
like, not the worst.

00:27:47.249 --> 00:27:49.540
For being kind of random, I
threw some numbers together

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:52.610
and was like, I'll take it.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:53.940
Let's see.

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:56.960
Yeah, and then just a brief
look at some predictions.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.640
Here's one such example.

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:00.590
This is the letter d,
in case it wasn't clear.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:04.700
It's like an upside-down p.

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:07.880
And when we load it in--

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:08.949
sorry, this one.

00:28:08.949 --> 00:28:10.740
I think I might have
just been visualizing.

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:12.800
And then I decided
to, instead of trying

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:16.650
to pick numbers myself, I
just picked 10 random ones.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.840
These are the 10
that happened to run.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.920
So the predictions
are on the first row,

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:23.930
and the labels are
in the second--

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:25.140
the correct label.

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:28.160
So you can see we
got some wrong.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.310
We got most of them right.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:30.920
We got that one wrong,
we got that one-- that's

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:31.760
the same one that got wrong.

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:32.610
Look at that.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:36.170
So whatever 41 is,
this g, it keeps

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:38.405
thinking that whatever
44 is actually a g.

00:28:38.405 --> 00:28:41.330
So if 41 is g--

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.820
a, b, c, d, f, g, h, i, j.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:47.550
So j's and g's, you could see
how that might look similar.

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:50.750
And for a lowly
trained CNN model

00:28:50.750 --> 00:28:52.584
it might struggle
in that department.

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:58.460
Here's an n, for those
who were wondering

00:28:58.460 --> 00:28:59.630
what the n's look like.

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:03.890
It kind of looks like an
upside-down n for some reason.

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:06.650
Some more letters.

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:08.120
Anyways, so that's prediction.

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:12.500
So far everything we've see
is kind of your standard Keras

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:13.200
stuff, right?

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.950
There's nothing
that we've really

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:20.540
looked at that's super
wacky, or you know, aside

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:24.950
from TensorFlow Import
Keras, everything else so far

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:28.130
could have just been how to
train a convolutional network

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.840
using Keras.

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:34.660
So let's see, if we move on
from the kind of creating

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:38.570
model training and
such, we get to some

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:40.440
of the interesting pieces here.

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:43.820
So Keras.estimator, this
is one of the pieces

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:46.890
that were added to
Keras in the version

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:49.340
that inside of TensorFlow.

00:29:49.340 --> 00:29:53.300
So normal Keras does not
have a dot estimator module.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:56.759
And so you have this
estimator.model to estimator.

00:29:56.759 --> 00:29:58.300
Basically it's a
conversion function.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:01.460
It's a utility function that
will take your Keras model.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:05.640
And right now I have the
model as a Python pointer.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.460
I just have that in a variable.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:10.280
You could also-- if you
had a Keras model that

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:12.740
had been previously exported
there's a different argument.

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:15.670
I think it's like Keras
directory, or something.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:18.531
For the file name, you can pass
in the exported file directly.

00:30:18.531 --> 00:30:20.780
You wouldn't have to load
it first and then put it in.

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:22.280
It'll take care of that for you.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:25.580
And so you could also get a
model that someone else is

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:27.242
giving you, or saying
like, hey, I've

00:30:27.242 --> 00:30:28.950
got this thing if you
want to try it out.

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:31.790
And you're like,
oh, I want to do xyz

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:33.770
but use TensorFlow estimators.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:34.940
Go for it.

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:39.830
And so then once
you have the model,

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:42.360
the estimator does
need a place to live.

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:44.840
So this model directory
is the directory

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:50.480
for the estimator model, not the
directory for the Keras model.

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.709
So that's why I tried
to give it a good name,

00:30:52.709 --> 00:30:54.750
like estimator model, so
I wouldn't get confused.

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:02.140
And the only other side note
I would give here is that,

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:05.757
when you use this-- if you
were to use this to convert

00:31:05.757 --> 00:31:07.840
to an estimator and then
actually do more training

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:10.090
on it-- which we could, but
we're not going to in this

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:10.850
case--

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:14.590
the input functions
that you use need

00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:18.505
to have kind of a name
for what to call the--

00:31:21.034 --> 00:31:22.075
I forget the proper term.

00:31:22.075 --> 00:31:25.510
But basically there's the key
to the dictionary of inputs.

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:28.840
And that key is com2d
input in this case.

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:33.760
So you grab it from
the model.input names

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:35.555
that's coming from
the Keras model,

00:31:35.555 --> 00:31:37.180
because you couldn't
explicitly set it.

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:42.200
Because it was built from
Keras, Keras sets it for you.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:45.730
So next we're going to take
the TensorFlow estimator model,

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:48.830
and now we need to
export it as a file.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.500
So to do that it's
a little bit more

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:54.040
involved, because what
we're going to do is we're

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:58.840
going to set it up that we
can read in JPEGs and PNG

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:02.650
images directly, rather than
reading in a row of a CSV file,

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:04.260
because that's not an image.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:05.587
We built a CNN.

00:32:05.587 --> 00:32:07.420
These are supposed to
be handwritten letters

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:08.460
and numbers.

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:14.470
So if you were to imagine
writing a production

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:19.690
service which had
to do this task,

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:23.860
wouldn't you be
sending images encoded

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:28.300
over the wire and not
an array of numbers

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:31.167
encoded as an array
with commas between?

00:32:31.167 --> 00:32:33.250
Like, it would just be
terribly sized inefficient.

00:32:33.250 --> 00:32:35.770
So you would encode
it using Base64

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:39.350
on a JPEG, or Base64 on a PNG,
and send that over the wire.

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:42.580
And so to take that and
then remunge it back

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:48.490
to a long array of
numbers, manually no less,

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:50.370
just doesn't feel right.

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:52.870
And so what I would
recommend here doing is,

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:54.430
TensorFlow has a
number of utilities

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:58.810
to help us create this
serving input function.

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:03.430
So the inputs to the model when
it's serving predictions can be

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:05.500
different-- can have kind
of a different head--

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:08.200
than what is used when
we're doing training.

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:11.330
So in our case, we did training
in Keras anyway, so that's done

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:12.610
and we're set.

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:14.960
And what I'm doing here--

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:17.440
I'll try to walk through
this as clearly as possible,

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:20.350
because it is easy to get lost--

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:25.910
is we used some raw TensorFlow
pieces here to get this done.

00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:28.510
So the first piece is the
TensorFlow placeholder.

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:31.090
A placeholder is basically
what it sounds like.

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:34.760
It holds the place for
where data will come in.

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:37.960
So this is where
the binary image--

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:41.740
the Base64 encoded byte string--

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:42.430
will land.

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:46.820
So it's a string, and
it is zero dimensions.

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:49.150
And so we're calling
this input_ph. ph

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:51.340
is short for placeholder here.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.710
And what we're going to do with
that is we're going to call

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:57.790
TF.decode_images.

00:33:57.790 --> 00:34:02.050
And what that does is,
it will take an image

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:09.230
and decode it into, in this
case, unsigned int8 array of--

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:09.900
a numpy array.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:12.260
A tensor of unsigned int8.

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:15.070
And so there's a couple
of things happening here,

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:18.580
but at the core of it
this is what is notable.

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:22.300
We're saying, from
here decode this.

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:25.179
And what we're calling to
make that happen is map.

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:28.719
So we're going to
use map to map decode

00:34:28.719 --> 00:34:33.570
onto whatever is falling
into the input placeholder.

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:38.049
Decode image, however takes
as a default argument channels

00:34:38.049 --> 00:34:38.920
is three.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:41.000
It's expecting a color image.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.580
But our images are grayscale.

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:44.020
There's only one channel.

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:47.830
And so because this argument
here, the first argument

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:52.270
of map, is a function,
not a function call,

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:56.679
I'm using the Python functools
to do a wrap around it.

00:34:56.679 --> 00:34:59.950
And it's essentially
a lambda where I just

00:34:59.950 --> 00:35:01.040
set the channels to one.

00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:03.430
So now when it
calls this, channels

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:06.875
will be set to one by default.
And so it's a little bit

00:35:06.875 --> 00:35:07.375
convoluted.

00:35:07.375 --> 00:35:08.810
I probably could
have broken that out

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:10.185
into a separate
line, what I just

00:35:10.185 --> 00:35:12.940
highlighted, and just called
that, like, custom decode

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:14.260
image.

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:17.570
And so we say map
function our decode image

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:22.970
onto input placeholder, and the
output type is unsigned int8.

00:35:22.970 --> 00:35:26.200
And then we're going to cast
that, because the defaults are

00:35:26.200 --> 00:35:32.740
this, and we do want it to use
a TensorFlow cast to float 32,

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:33.880
and then of course--

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:35.420
I almost forgot to do this--

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:37.810
divide by 255.

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:38.860
All right.

00:35:38.860 --> 00:35:40.290
So that's kind of our images.

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:43.086
So we have
transformed our input,

00:35:43.086 --> 00:35:45.460
which was just a placeholder
because we don't know what's

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:53.440
coming in yet, into a grayscale
image that was divided by 255.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:56.412
The final thing we
need to do is to know

00:35:56.412 --> 00:36:01.120
that decode image actually is
smart and knows the dimensions

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:02.150
of an image coming in.

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:04.410
And so the image
that it spits out,

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:09.830
or the 2D array that it spits
out, will be those dimensions.

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:12.562
And because of that, we do
not know at compile time,

00:36:12.562 --> 00:36:14.770
so to speak, what the
dimensions of an image will be,

00:36:14.770 --> 00:36:17.600
because that happens at runtime.

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:20.454
This runs into a
problem later on where,

00:36:20.454 --> 00:36:22.870
when the model tries to export,
it says, I don't know what

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.620
dimensions you're going to use.

00:36:24.620 --> 00:36:26.530
And so that's why we
need this final line

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:28.060
where we set the shape.

00:36:28.060 --> 00:36:31.327
This isn't explicitly changing
the image, it's more of,

00:36:31.327 --> 00:36:32.910
you think of it as
like an annotation.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:35.510
Is It's kind of a, let me give
you some more information--

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:36.430
some more details.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:39.070
It's like metadata--
and tag it as none,

00:36:39.070 --> 00:36:40.180
which is the batch size.

00:36:40.180 --> 00:36:41.620
You can pass in
three images, you

00:36:41.620 --> 00:36:44.044
can pass in 70 images,
one image, whatever.

00:36:44.044 --> 00:36:45.460
And then it's going
to be 28 by 28

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:47.390
by one, because we
have one channel.

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:48.970
It's grayscale.

00:36:48.970 --> 00:36:54.010
And then we're going to do,
we have input placeholder

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:55.580
and we have images.

00:36:55.580 --> 00:36:57.010
Those are our two things.

00:36:57.010 --> 00:36:59.620
And we're going to use that--
here we have images and input

00:36:59.620 --> 00:37:00.119
placeholder.

00:37:00.119 --> 00:37:02.230
We're going to use
these two as arguments

00:37:02.230 --> 00:37:04.670
to the serving input receiver.

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:06.400
And this is a wrapper
utility function

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:10.140
that would generate
what we need to tack on

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:13.600
to the top of our model to
use as an input function

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:16.550
for serving predictions.

00:37:16.550 --> 00:37:20.200
And so I'm choosing
to use the model input

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:22.630
name, that com2d_input.

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:26.680
And the second argument I
would recommend using bytes.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:31.570
And we'll see why, but just
believe me for now that we'll

00:37:31.570 --> 00:37:33.460
use bytes as the argument.

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:35.570
But you could
theoretically change it.

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:39.430
But if you do change it,
it should end in _bytes.

00:37:39.430 --> 00:37:42.190
So bytes is fine.

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:45.010
And then when we pass in here
is images and the placeholder.

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:47.380
So basically it's saying,
the first argument

00:37:47.380 --> 00:37:50.880
is what will be
going into the model,

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:53.860
and the second argument
is what is coming

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:56.110
in at the top before that--

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.620
the placeholder.

