01:13:25.580 --> 01:13:29.030
Is, if my understanding
is correct,

01:13:29.030 --> 01:13:32.060
more based around the
fact that should you

01:13:32.060 --> 01:13:37.190
want to then move
to a larger dataset,

01:13:37.190 --> 01:13:40.450
maybe several GPUs hooked
up to the same machine,

01:13:40.450 --> 01:13:43.790
moving to any number
of cloud environments,

01:13:43.790 --> 01:13:46.100
those sorts of things
then become a lot easier

01:13:46.100 --> 01:13:49.190
and become basically
seamless if you already

01:13:49.190 --> 01:13:51.440
have a TensorFlow estimator.

01:13:51.440 --> 01:13:55.910
And there's also certain
benefits in terms of the code.

01:13:55.910 --> 01:13:57.920
The conversion can be
made a little bit easier

01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:00.587
in certain spots if you
convert it and train it as-is

01:14:00.587 --> 01:14:02.420
rather than trying to
convert it afterwards.

01:14:02.420 --> 01:14:05.724
PUNEITH KAUL: There's
actually one more benefit.

01:14:05.724 --> 01:14:08.961
So how many of you have
heard of [INAUDIBLE]??

01:14:08.961 --> 01:14:10.960
It actually uses this
strategy called allreduce.

01:14:10.960 --> 01:14:14.430
So TensorFlow now has in
contrib a distribution strategy.

01:14:14.430 --> 01:14:17.090
So you basically
can tell TensorFlow

01:14:17.090 --> 01:14:22.925
whether you want to use a PSS,
or workers, or allreduce, ring

01:14:22.925 --> 01:14:26.000
allreduce, which is a
much better technique

01:14:26.000 --> 01:14:27.730
to do distribution.

01:14:27.730 --> 01:14:32.930
And that you can plug in with
the estimator very seamlessly.

01:14:32.930 --> 01:14:36.320
So that's another
benefit of doing that.

01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:40.760
YUFENG GUO: It seems like the
push is really around keeping

01:14:40.760 --> 01:14:44.600
