benefits both ways.

01:15:45.320 --> 01:15:51.560
Like, on one side Keras has
an arguably simpler API,

01:15:51.560 --> 01:15:56.750
and perhaps fewer concepts
that you have to learn.

01:15:56.750 --> 01:15:59.730
So I guess part of it
is the question of,

01:15:59.730 --> 01:16:02.850
do you already know one of these
two libraries going into it.

01:16:02.850 --> 01:16:05.580
And so there's a
different comparison.

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:07.950
If you say, I know Keras
and I know TensorFlow.

01:16:07.950 --> 01:16:09.300
Which one should I use?

01:16:09.300 --> 01:16:12.477
Versus, I don't know either,
which one should I learn?

01:16:12.477 --> 01:16:13.310
Which one is better?

01:16:13.310 --> 01:16:14.230
Which is faster?

01:16:14.230 --> 01:16:18.030
In those two use
cases, it can kind of

01:16:18.030 --> 01:16:20.637
lead to different
considerations.

01:16:20.637 --> 01:16:22.470
And then of course
there's the consideration

01:16:22.470 --> 01:16:23.970
of your actual data format.

01:16:23.970 --> 01:16:29.160
I think at this point both
libraries are reasonably clean,

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:33.180
but as Puneith mentioned,
going forward the

01:16:33.180 --> 01:16:37.290
officially supported and
developed and promoted method

01:16:37.290 --> 01:16:40.980
of working with TensorFlow will
be through Keras, especially

01:16:40.980 --> 01:16:44.070
for the model authoring step
where you construct the model

01:16:44.070 --> 01:16:46.110
structure.

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:50.520
So TensorFlow 1.9 just
came out a few days ago,

01:16:50.520 --> 01:16:57.920
and it comes with a total
refresh of the TensorFlow

01:16:57.920 --> 01:17:03.600
website in terms of the guides,
the Getting Started guides.

01:17:03.600 --> 01:17:07.930
They are now all pivoted
to Keras and Eager mode,

01:17:07.930 --> 01:17:09.340
which we didn't talk about here.

01:17:09.340 --> 01:17:11.960
But we'll just skip that point.

01:17:11.960 --> 01:17:13.720
Keras.

01:17:13.720 --> 01:17:19.760
So basically Keras,
and more Keras.

01:17:23.630 --> 01:17:25.250
Cool, yeah?

01:17:25.250 --> 01:17:27.530
AUDIENCE: The data
[INAUDIBLE] mentioned, of this

01:17:27.530 --> 01:17:30.520
is how we're get a clean
dataset, you have it.

01:17:30.520 --> 01:17:33.538
But in real life
we get data, then

01:17:33.538 --> 01:17:35.823
we get some more
data, [INAUDIBLE]..

01:17:35.823 --> 01:17:37.145
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, absolutely.

01:17:37.145 --> 01:17:38.520
AUDIENCE: So in
a version control

01:17:38.520 --> 01:17:40.537
we talk about [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:40.537 --> 01:17:43.411
I mean that's just the code.

01:17:43.411 --> 01:17:45.865
But at the end of the
model you went to what

01:17:45.865 --> 01:17:47.750
data was used in the model.

01:17:47.750 --> 01:17:53.150
A lot of these data signs were
[INAUDIBLE] for collaboration.

01:17:53.150 --> 01:17:56.130
Are there, like, some of
these collaboration tools

01:17:56.130 --> 01:17:59.562
within Google Cloud?

01:17:59.562 --> 01:18:01.977
Or do you have any
recommendations for which ones

01:18:01.977 --> 01:18:04.170
we should use?

01:18:04.170 --> 01:18:11.536
[INAUDIBLE]

01:18:11.536 --> 01:18:12.910
YUFENG GUO: Yeah,
so the question

01:18:12.910 --> 01:18:16.740
is around the difficulties
behind versioning

01:18:16.740 --> 01:18:21.280
datasets and collaboration
of training models

01:18:21.280 --> 01:18:24.250
on differing versions
of those data,

01:18:24.250 --> 01:18:28.520
as well as collaborating
on the source code itself--

01:18:28.520 --> 01:18:30.550
which perhaps
software engineering

01:18:30.550 --> 01:18:35.530
has developed more standards
around than for data.

01:18:35.530 --> 01:18:40.250
So I'll throw a few
ideas around here.

01:18:40.250 --> 01:18:43.540
One is that, in addition to the
Kaggle kernels being versioned,

01:18:43.540 --> 01:18:45.440
the datasets are
actually also versioned.

01:18:45.440 --> 01:18:47.800
So you can create new
versions of your dataset.

01:18:47.800 --> 01:18:50.200
And when you combine
that with the notion

01:18:50.200 --> 01:18:52.510
that you can have
private datasets that

01:18:52.510 --> 01:18:56.710
are shared among a
selected group of users,

01:18:56.710 --> 01:19:00.627
then you can fit together
a decent collaboration

01:19:00.627 --> 01:19:02.960
model in that sense, because
you have version notebooks,

01:19:02.960 --> 01:19:06.400
you have version datasets,
both with ability

01:19:06.400 --> 01:19:08.620
to do access control.

01:19:08.620 --> 01:19:12.580
That being said,
obviously Kaggle kernels

01:19:12.580 --> 01:19:16.980
are not going to serve
all your production needs.

01:19:16.980 --> 01:19:20.500
It's a great learning and
collaborative platform,

01:19:20.500 --> 01:19:24.970
but when it comes time to deploy
your model-- when you're not

01:19:24.970 --> 01:19:27.520
just competing in a
competition or doing something

01:19:27.520 --> 01:19:30.610
for fun, then you
kind of end up having

01:19:30.610 --> 01:19:34.510
to sometimes look elsewhere.

01:19:34.510 --> 01:19:36.440
You mentioned CoLab
earlier today.

01:19:36.440 --> 01:19:39.290
And so in CoLab
what's neat about it--

01:19:39.290 --> 01:19:43.300
and I forgot to mention this in
the pro-con list by accident,

01:19:43.300 --> 01:19:45.370
I assure you, because
it is a neat feature.

01:19:45.370 --> 01:19:50.020
CoLab is like Jupyter
notebooks, Google Docs style.

01:19:50.020 --> 01:19:51.760
And I mean that in its entirety.

01:19:51.760 --> 01:19:55.600
In particular, it lets you
do simultaneous editing

01:19:55.600 --> 01:19:57.090
just like in Google Docs.

01:19:57.090 --> 01:19:59.470
So if I'm in the CoLab,
and you're in the CoLab,

01:19:59.470 --> 01:20:02.850
we are both running against the
same computer in the back end.

01:20:02.850 --> 01:20:04.720
It's like you and
I are sitting side

01:20:04.720 --> 01:20:07.104
by side at the same machine,
but we for some reason

01:20:07.104 --> 01:20:09.020
have two monitors and
the same file pulled up,

01:20:09.020 --> 01:20:11.279
so we can look at different
parts at the same time.

01:20:11.279 --> 01:20:12.820
And it means that
we can put comments

01:20:12.820 --> 01:20:14.380
in, just like in Google Docs.

01:20:14.380 --> 01:20:17.620
So you can actually add
comments to someone else's CoLab

01:20:17.620 --> 01:20:20.109
and say, hey, I don't
think it is quite right.

01:20:20.109 --> 01:20:21.400
Maybe you could try this thing.

01:20:21.400 --> 01:20:23.410
Or you could use
it to ask questions

01:20:23.410 --> 01:20:24.520
to your collaborators.

01:20:24.520 --> 01:20:28.450
And so there's a lot of
different pieces there.

01:20:31.000 --> 01:20:33.520
I don't know that
there's any good--

01:20:33.520 --> 01:20:38.950
all the things combined
under the sun in one package

01:20:38.950 --> 01:20:40.900
at this stage.

01:20:40.900 --> 01:20:45.400
I'm sure folks are working
to make that happen and get

01:20:45.400 --> 01:20:47.350
the advantages of
the different tools,

01:20:47.350 --> 01:20:50.800
and kind of put them in a
more unified manner to A,

01:20:50.800 --> 01:20:53.260
reduce confusion, but also
increase the functionality

01:20:53.260 --> 01:20:55.630
and improve the
workflows around this.

01:20:55.630 --> 01:20:57.370
So yeah, I mean that
kind of goes back

01:20:57.370 --> 01:20:59.470
to the central
point around this,

01:20:59.470 --> 01:21:02.290
that the tooling
is kind of still

01:21:02.290 --> 01:21:06.700
in development, just across
the field in general.

01:21:06.700 --> 01:21:11.330
There's new things happening all
the time, and for the most part

01:21:11.330 --> 01:21:13.120
those new things are better.

01:21:13.120 --> 01:21:17.290
Better things are coming and
they're coming at a rapid rate.

01:21:17.290 --> 01:21:22.130
And it's an exciting
time, for sure.

